/hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-discussion.html)\n-   [Metrics](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-metrics.html)\n-   [Related articles](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-relations.html)\n\nResearch article\n\n | **Highlight paper**\n\n | [![](https://contentmanager.copernicus.org/2175966/13/ssl)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/)\n\n20 Oct 2017\n\nResearch article | **Highlight paper** | [![](https://contentmanager.copernicus.org/2175966/13/ssl)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/) | 20 Oct 2017\n\n[![](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-avatar-thumb150.png)](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017-avatar-web.png)\n\n# The CAMELS data set: catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies\n\nNans Addor[](mailto:n.addor@uea.ac.uk), Andrew J. Newman, Naoki Mizukami, and Martyn P. Clark\n\n### Nans Addor\n\nCORRESPONDING AUTHOR\n\n[n.addor@uea.ac.uk](mailto:n.addor@uea.ac.uk)\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6057-3930](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6057-3930)\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\nnow at: Climatic Research Unit, School of Environmental Sciences, University of East Anglia, Norwich, UK\n\n### Andrew J. Newman\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8796-0861](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8796-0861)\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\n### Naoki Mizukami\n\n[https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0893-5869](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0893-5869)\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\n### Martyn P. Clark\n\n×\n\nResearch Applications Laboratory, National Center for Atmospheric Research, Boulder, CO, USA\n\n**Abstract.** We present a new data set of attributes for 671 catchments in the contiguous United States (CONUS) minimally impacted by human activities. This complements the daily time series of meteorological forcing and streamflow provided by Newman et al. (2015b). To produce this extension, we synthesized diverse and complementary data sets to describe six main classes of attributes at the catchment scale: topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology. The spatial variations among basins over the CONUS are discussed and compared using a series of maps. The large number of catchments, combined with the diversity of the attributes we extracted, makes this new data set well suited for large-sample studies and comparative hydrology. In comparison to the similar Model Parameter Estimation Experiment (MOPEX) data set, this data set relies on more recent data, it covers a wider range of attributes, and its catchments are more evenly distributed across the CONUS. This study also involves assessments of the limitations of the source data sets used to compute catchment attributes, as well as detailed descriptions of how the attributes were computed. The hydrometeorological time series provided by Newman et al. (2015b, [https://doi.org/10.5065/D6MW2F4D](https://doi.org/10.5065/D6MW2F4D)) together with the catchment attributes introduced in this paper ([https://doi.org/10.5065/D6G73C3Q](https://doi.org/10.5065/D6G73C3Q)) constitute the freely available CAMELS data set, which stands for Catchment Attributes and MEteorology for Large-sample Studies.\n\nDownload & links\n\n-   [Article (PDF, 8761 KB)](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.pdf \"PDF Version (8761 KB)\")\n\nDownload & links\n\n-   [Article](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.pdf \"PDF Version (8761 KB)\") (8761 KB)\n-   [Full-text XML](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.xml \"XML Version\")\n-   [BibTeX](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.bib)\n-   [EndNote](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.ris)\n\nShare\n\n[![Mendeley](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/mendeley.png)](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Mendeley\")\n\n[![Reddit](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/reddit.png)](https://www.reddit.com/submit?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Reddit\")\n\n[![Twitter](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/twitter.png)](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Twitter\")\n\n[![Facebook](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/facebook.png)](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&t=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"Facebook\")\n\n[![LinkedIn](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/linkedin.png)](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&title=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"LinkedIn\")\n\n[](#)\n\n**How to cite.** Addor, N., Newman, A. J., Mizukami, N., and Clark, M. P.: The CAMELS data set: catchment attributes and meteorology for large-sample studies, Hydrol. Earth Syst. Sci., 21, 5293–5313, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-5293-2017, 2017.\n\nReceived: 22 Mar 2017 – Discussion started: 30 Mar 2017 – Revised: 04 Sep 2017 – Accepted: 08 Sep 2017 – Published: 20 Oct 2017\n\nDownload\n\n-   [Article](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.pdf \"PDF Version (8761 KB)\") (8761 KB)\n-   [Full-text XML](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.xml \"XML Version\")\n\n-   [BibTeX](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.bib)\n-   [EndNote](https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.ris)\n\nShort summary\n\nWe introduce a data set describing the landscape of 671 catchments in the contiguous USA: we synthesized various data sources to characterize the topography, climate, streamflow, land cover, soil, and geology of each catchment. This extends the daily time series of meteorological forcing and discharge provided by an earlier study. The diversity of these catchments will help to improve our understanding and modeling of how the interplay between catchment attributes shapes hydrological processes.\n\nWe introduce a data set describing the landscape of 671 catchments in the contiguous USA: we...\n\n[Read more](#)\n\nShare\n\n[![Mendeley](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/mendeley.png)](https://www.mendeley.com/import/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Mendeley\")\n\n[![Reddit](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/reddit.png)](https://www.reddit.com/submit?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Reddit\")\n\n[![Twitter](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/twitter.png)](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F \"Twitter\")\n\n[![Facebook](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/facebook.png)](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&t=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"Facebook\")\n\n[![LinkedIn](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/linkedin.png)](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&url=https%3A%2F%2Fhess.copernicus.org%2Farticles%2F21%2F5293%2F2017%2F&title=The+CAMELS+data+set%3A+catchment+attributes+and+meteorology+for+large-sample+studies \"LinkedIn\")\n\n[](#)\n\nHydrology and Earth System Sciences\n\nAn interactive open-access journal of the European Geosciences Union\n\n[![](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/graphic_egu_logo_white.png)](http://www.egu.eu/)\n\n[![](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/template_logo_copernicus_publications_177x22_white.png)](https://publications.copernicus.org/)\n\n[](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)All site content, except where otherwise noted, is licensed under the [Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).\n\n[Contact](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/about/contact.html)\n\n|\n\n[Imprint](https://www.hydrology-and-earth-system-sciences.net/imprint.html)\n\n|\n\n[Data protection](https://www.copernicus.org/data_protection.html)","metadata":{"truncated":false},"title":"https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.html (text/html)","time":{"start":1792454533355,"end":1792454533637}},"id":"prt_1569d9fdb001oa4fC21sf0p7VI","sessionID":"ses_ea962aa48ffe7fg2I6fW6DcPEb","messageID":"msg_1569d681f0015qZjKeBlHSvYFv"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792454534089,"sessionID":"ses_ea962aa48ffe7fg2I6fW6DcPEb","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"webfetch","callID":"call_01_Wnctg5tSklSYy2bkZiUb7261","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"url":"https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/","format":"markdown"},"output":"     Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures - Illinois Experts             \n\n[Skip to main navigation](#main-navigation) [Skip to search](#main-search) [Skip to main content](#main-content)\n\n[Illinois Experts Home ![Illinois Experts Logo](/skin/headerImage/)](/en/) \n\n-   [LOGIN & Help   Link opens in a new tab](http://go.illinois.edu/experts)\n\nSearch content at Illinois Experts \n\n-   [Home](/en/)\n-   [Profiles](/en/persons/)\n-   [Research units](/en/organisations/)\n-   [Research & Scholarship](/en/publications/)\n-   [Datasets](/en/datasets/)\n-   [Honors](/en/prizes/)\n-   [Press/Media](/en/clippings/)\n-   [Activities](/en/activities/)\n\n# Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\n\n-   Henrique R.A. Freitas\n-   , [Celso L. Mendes](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/)\n-   , Aleksandar Ilic\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\n[](https://plu.mx/plum/a/?doi=10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\n-   [Overview](/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/)\n-   [Fingerprint](/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/fingerprints/)\n\n## Abstract\n\nLarge-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB (“Modelo de Grandes Bacias”) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6% for discharges and objective functions.\n\nOriginal language\n\nEnglish (US)\n\nArticle number\n\n105271\n\nJournal\n\n[Environmental Modelling and Software](#)\n\nVolume\n\n148\n\nDOIs\n\n-   [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\nState\n\nPublished - Feb 2022\n\nExternally published\n\nYes\n\n## Keywords\n\n-   High performance computing\n-   Hydrology models\n-   Parallel processing\n-   Parameterization\n-   Roofline model\n-   Vectorization\n\n## ASJC Scopus subject areas\n\n-   Software\n-   Environmental Engineering\n-   Ecological Modeling\n\n## Online availability\n\n-   [10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n    \n\n## Library availability\n\n[Discover UIUC Full Text](https://i-share-uiu.primo.exlibrisgroup.com/openurl/01CARLI_UIU/01CARLI_UIU:CARLI_UIU?ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&ctx_tim=2026-10-19T06%3A44%3A15UTC&ctx_enc=info%3Aofi%2FencUTF-8&url_ver=Z39.88-2004&url_ctx_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Actx&rft.genre=article&rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Fkev%3Afmt%3Ajournal&rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fpure.atira.dk%3Apure&rft.atitle=Performance%20optimization%20of%20the%20MGB%20hydrological%20model%20for%20multi-core%20and%20GPU%20architectures&rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1016%2Fj.envsoft.2021.105271&rft.aulast=Freitas&rft.aufirst=Henrique%20R.A.&rft.auinit=H&rft.date=2022-02&rft.volume=148&rft.issn=1364-8152&rft.jtitle=Environmental%20Modelling%20and%20Software)\n\n## Related links\n\n-   [Link to publication in Scopus](https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432)\n    \n-   [Link to the citations in Scopus](https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432#tab=citedBy)\n    \n\n## Fingerprint\n\nDive into the research topics of 'Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n\n-   Multicore Architecture Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Performance Optimization Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Hydrological Model Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Multi-GPU Architectures Keyphrases 100%\n    \n-   Multicore Computer Science 100%\n    \n-   Graphics Processing Unit Computer Science 100%\n    \n-   Calibration Method Keyphrases 50%\n    \n-   Computational Resources Keyphrases 50%\n    \n\n[View full fingerprint](/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/fingerprints/)\n\n## Cite this\n\n-   APA\n-   Standard\n-   Harvard\n-   Vancouver\n-   Author\n-   BIBTEX\n-   RIS\n\nFreitas, H. R. A.[, Mendes, C. L.](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/), & Ilic, A. (2022). [Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/). *Environmental Modelling and Software*, *148*, Article 105271. [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\n[**Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures.**](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/) / Freitas, Henrique R.A.[; Mendes, Celso L.](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/); Ilic, Aleksandar.  \nIn: Environmental Modelling and Software, Vol. 148, 105271, 02.2022.\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\nFreitas, HRA[, Mendes, CL](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/) & Ilic, A 2022, '[Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/)', *Environmental Modelling and Software*, vol. 148, 105271. [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271)\n\nFreitas HRA[, Mendes CL](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/), Ilic A. [Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/). Environmental Modelling and Software. 2022 Feb;148:105271. doi: 10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\n\nFreitas, Henrique R.A. [; Mendes, Celso L.](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/persons/celso-luiz-mendes/) ; Ilic, Aleksandar. / [**Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures**](https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/). In: [Environmental Modelling and Software](#). 2022 ; Vol. 148.\n\n@article{19ce0bb9118545d6ad2a55bfe8d415ce,\n\ntitle = \"Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\",\n\nabstract = \"Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB ({\\\\textquotedblleft}Modelo de Grandes Bacias{\\\\textquotedblright}) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 {\\\\texttimes} (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 {\\\\texttimes} (CPU) and 65 {\\\\texttimes} (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6\\\\% for discharges and objective functions.\",\n\nkeywords = \"High performance computing, Hydrology models, Parallel processing, Parameterization, Roofline model, Vectorization\",\n\nauthor = \"Freitas, \\\\{Henrique R.A.\\\\} and Mendes, \\\\{Celso L.\\\\} and Aleksandar Ilic\",\n\nnote = \"Modern CPUs support vector extensions up to 512 bits (current Intel CPUs) for Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) data-parallel strategies. Compilers are not fully effective in automatic vectorization, so hand-tuned explicitly vectorized codes execute faster than the auto-vectorized equivalents. However, explicit vectorization requires careful and detailed code analysis (low-level instructions), and more development time (Mitra et al., 2013). The additional availability of multiple cores in CPUs (Ralston, 2008) also enables thread-parallel strategies in shared-memory environments. The workload is distributed to multiple CPU cores, identified as threads, and processed in parallel. The OpenMP standard (Dagum and Menon, 1998) executes applications with thread-level parallelism via compiler directives that dynamically activate/deactivate parallel threads.The STE, DIS, and CON routines were executed on GPU as CUDA kernels with the CUDA Fortran interface (NVIDIA, 2020a), supported by the PGI pgf90 compiler (PGI, 2020). The CUDA framework was chosen because we previously applied directive-based GPU programming standards (OpenACC) on the MGB model (Freitas and Mendes, 2019), but that particular optimization decreased the performance, mainly caused by overheads associated to the OpenACC directives that increased runtimes from millions of routine calls, not amortized by thousands of loop iterations (catchments), i.e. computations were not GPU intensive.The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Cient{\\\\'i}fico e Tecnol{\\\\'o}gico (CNPq), Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Funda{\\\\c c}{\\\\~a}o para a Ci{\\\\^e}ncia e a Tecnologia (FCT), under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017. The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Cient{\\\\'i}fico e Tecnol{\\\\'o}gico (CNPq) , Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Funda{\\\\c c}{\\\\~a}o para a Ci{\\\\^e}ncia e a Tecnologia (FCT) , under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017.\",\n\nyear = \"2022\",\n\nmonth = feb,\n\ndoi = \"10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\",\n\nlanguage = \"English (US)\",\n\nvolume = \"148\",\n\njournal = \"Environmental Modelling and Software\",\n\nissn = \"1364-8152\",\n\npublisher = \"Elsevier Ltd\",\n\n}\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\n\nAU - Freitas, Henrique R.A.\n\nAU - Mendes, Celso L.\n\nAU - Ilic, Aleksandar\n\nN1 - Modern CPUs support vector extensions up to 512 bits (current Intel CPUs) for Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) data-parallel strategies. Compilers are not fully effective in automatic vectorization, so hand-tuned explicitly vectorized codes execute faster than the auto-vectorized equivalents. However, explicit vectorization requires careful and detailed code analysis (low-level instructions), and more development time (Mitra et al., 2013). The additional availability of multiple cores in CPUs (Ralston, 2008) also enables thread-parallel strategies in shared-memory environments. The workload is distributed to multiple CPU cores, identified as threads, and processed in parallel. The OpenMP standard (Dagum and Menon, 1998) executes applications with thread-level parallelism via compiler directives that dynamically activate/deactivate parallel threads.The STE, DIS, and CON routines were executed on GPU as CUDA kernels with the CUDA Fortran interface (NVIDIA, 2020a), supported by the PGI pgf90 compiler (PGI, 2020). The CUDA framework was chosen because we previously applied directive-based GPU programming standards (OpenACC) on the MGB model (Freitas and Mendes, 2019), but that particular optimization decreased the performance, mainly caused by overheads associated to the OpenACC directives that increased runtimes from millions of routine calls, not amortized by thousands of loop iterations (catchments), i.e. computations were not GPU intensive.The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico (CNPq), Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia (FCT), under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017. The authors are grateful to Walter Collischonn from IPH-UFRGS for the availability of the MGB hydrological model and datasets. This work was financially supported by the Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico (CNPq) , Brazil, grant number 140 687/2 016-5; and by Portuguese national funds through Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia (FCT) , under projects UIDB/50 021/2 020 and PTDC/CCI-COM/31 901/2 017.\n\nPY - 2022/2\n\nY1 - 2022/2\n\nN2 - Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB (“Modelo de Grandes Bacias”) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6% for discharges and objective functions.\n\nAB - Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes with applications in water resources, climate change, land use, and forecast systems. The quality of the simulations mainly depends on calibrating optimal sets of watershed parameters, a time-consuming task that highly demands computational resources from repeated simulations. This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB (“Modelo de Grandes Bacias”) hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution) calibration method for two watersheds. The optimizations target state-of-the-art CPU/GPU systems, exploiting techniques that include AVX-512 vectorization, and multi-core (CPU) and many-core (GPU) parallelisms. Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration, while the scalability analysis indicated 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations with larger problem sizes. The roofline analysis confirmed more effective use of the hardware resources, and the quantitative accuracy evaluation of the optimized implementations reached maximum relative errors of approximately 6% for discharges and objective functions.\n\nKW - High performance computing\n\nKW - Hydrology models\n\nKW - Parallel processing\n\nKW - Parameterization\n\nKW - Roofline model\n\nKW - Vectorization\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85121208432#tab=citedBy\n\nU2 - 10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\n\nDO - 10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271\n\nM3 - Article\n\nAN - SCOPUS:85121208432\n\nSN - 1364-8152\n\nVL - 148\n\nJO - Environmental Modelling and Software\n\nJF - Environmental Modelling and Software\n\nM1 - 105271\n\nER -\n\n-   [](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/%3Futm_source%3Dfacebook%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dsharelink%26doi%3D10.1016%2Fj.envsoft.2021.105271&p[summary]=Check+out+this+research+output+at+Illinois+Experts%3A+Performance+optimization+of+the+MGB+hydrological+model+for+multi-core+and+GPU+architectures)\n-   [](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/performance-optimization-of-the-mgb-hydrological-model-for-multi-/%3Futm_source%3Dtwitter%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dsharelink%26doi%3D10.1016%2Fj.envsoft.2021.105271&text=Check+out+this+research+output+at+Illinois+Experts%3A+Performance+optimi